   373	-- function (not callable directly) and EXECUTE is revoked from PUBLIC, so no app role can attach it to a table of its own.
   374	-- Inside, current_user is the owner. The invoking role is the 'role' setting (changed only by SET ROLE, and only to a role the session
   375	-- belongs to; a security-definer function cannot change it), and must equal the current_user that the guard trigger recorded with the
   376	-- caller's rights for this update. Any other role, or a disagreement, raises.
   377	-- That check alone is not a proof. The statement can still change both values after the guard ran and before this AFTER trigger fires
   378	-- (RETURNING set_config('role',…), set_config('app.outbox_invoker',…)); they then agree on a role the session can also take. The
   379	-- actor is therefore proven only under an invariant on memberships: no login is a member of both libetech_runtime and libetech_intake,
   380	-- and no role is a member of libetech_outbox_audit or has it as a member (Database review I-1). 016 and 017 refuse to apply when it
   381	-- is broken, src/server/db.ts refuses to start a restricted login that breaks it, and this trigger and the validator above refuse a
   382	-- state change by a login that breaks it, for memberships granted after the migrations ran. Superuser sessions are outside the proof.
   383	CREATE OR REPLACE FUNCTION public.outbox_messages_record_state_event() RETURNS trigger LANGUAGE plpgsql SECURITY DEFINER
   384	SET search_path = pg_catalog, public, pg_temp AS $$
   385	DECLARE sig text; req text; invoker text; recorded text; kind text; who uuid;
   386	BEGIN
   387	  sig := nullif(pg_catalog.current_setting('app.outbox_signal', true), '');
   388	  req := nullif(pg_catalog.current_setting('app.request_id', true), '');
   389	  IF sig IS NULL OR req IS NULL THEN RAISE EXCEPTION 'Outbox state change needs app.outbox_signal and app.request_id'; END IF;
